      "text": "I now have everything I need to give you a thorough response. Here is the full breakdown across all five tasks:\n\n---\n\n## 1. 📖 Publication History, Reception & Controversy\n\n**Publication History:**\n- **Title:** *To Kill a Mockingbird*\n- **Author:** Harper Lee\n- **Published:** July 11, 1960\n- **Publisher:** J.B. Lippincott & Co.\n- **ISBN:** 978-0-06-112008-4\n- Became an immediate bestseller and won the **Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1961** — just one year after publication.\n- Has never gone out of print and has sold over **40 million copies worldwide**.\n\n**Reception:**\n- Praised widely upon release for its moral clarity, its portrayal of racial injustice in the Jim Crow South, and its distinctive child narrator.\n- Adapted into an acclaimed 1962 film starring Gregory Peck as Atticus Finch, which won three Academy Awards.\n- Consistently ranks among the most beloved American novels (e.g., top of the BBC's and Library of Congress's most influential books lists).\n\n**Controversy:**\n- One of the **most frequently banned and challenged books** in the United States (American Library Association). Reasons include: racial slurs and language, depictions of rape, and concerns about whether it adequately centers Black perspectives vs. the \"white savior\" narrative.\n- More recently, scholars like **Toni Morrison** have critiqued the novel's framing — that the moral growth of white characters is privileged over the experience of Black ones.\n- In **2015**, the companion/prequel *Go Set a Watchman* was published, revealing a more morally complicated Atticus Finch, reigniting debate about the original novel's idealization of him.\n- Removed from school reading lists in several districts (e.g., Biloxi, MS in 2017) due to language.\n\n> **Syllabus Note:** This controversy is itself rich pedagogical material — pairing it with critical essays on the \"white savior\" framework (e.g., from Monroe, Boger, or Morrison) can generate strong discussion.\n\n---\n\n## 2. 📚 Plot Summary & Key Themes\n\n**Plot Summary:**\nSet in the fictional town of Maycomb, Alabama during the 1930s Great Depression era, the novel is narrated by **Jean Louise \"Scout\" Finch**, a young girl looking back on her childhood. The plot unfolds in two parts:\n\n- **Part One** follows Scout, her brother Jem, and their friend Dill as they become obsessed with their reclusive neighbor **Boo Radley**.\n- **Part Two** centers on Scout's father, **Atticus Finch**, a lawyer appointed to defend **Tom Robinson**, a Black man falsely accused of raping Mayella Ewell, a white woman. Despite a clear defense, Tom is convicted by an all-white jury. He is later shot and killed attempting to escape prison. Bob Ewell, the accuser's father, later attacks Scout and Jem — and Boo Radley rescues them.\n\n**Key Themes (from metadata + critical summary):**\n| Theme | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Racial Injustice** | The trial dramatizes how systemic racism overrides truth and evidence in the Jim Crow South. |\n| **Moral Courage** | Atticus models \"real courage\" — doing right even when you know you'll lose. |\n| **Childhood & Innocence** | Scout's coming-of-age narrative structures how readers encounter the novel's moral lessons. |\n| **Empathy** | \"You never really understand a person until you climb into their skin and walk around in it.\" |\n| **Class & Social Hierarchy** | Maycomb's rigid caste system (Finches, Cunninghams, Ewells, Black community) drives social conflict. |\n| **Southern Gothic** | The atmosphere of Maycomb, Boo Radley, and the decaying social order reflect the genre. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. 🌐 Online Availability\n\nBased on the search results and retrieval attempts, here is what was found:\n\n- *To Kill a Mockingbird* (1960) is **under active copyright** (©Harper Lee; rights now held by estate). It is **not in the public domain** and is not legally available through Project Gutenberg or similar open-access repositories.\n- Several sites appeared in search results claiming to host the full text or a PDF, but these are **unauthorized pirate copies** and would violate U.S. copyright law.\n- **Legal licensed access options** for your institution include:\n  - **HathiTrust Digital Library** (limited view for in-copyright works)\n  - **ProQuest Ebook Central** or **EBSCO eBook Collection** (via library subscription)\n  - **OverDrive/Libby** (public library ebook lending)\n  - **Amazon Kindle or Google Play** (purchase)\n  - Contact your **library acquisitions department** for a course e-reserve or digital license.\n\n---\n\n## 4. ⚖️ Trial Chapters (Tom Robinson Sequence)\n\nThe retrieval tool attempted to pull the Tom Robinson trial chapters, but returned text that does **not match** *To Kill a Mockingbird* — the excerpt features characters named \"Joshua,\" \"Arnold,\" and \"Mr. Ridley,\" which are not characters in the novel. This confirms the tool was unable to locate an authorized copy of the specific trial chapters.\n\n**What the trial sequence covers (Chapters 17–21):**\n- **Ch. 17–18:** Testimony of Bob Ewell and Mayella Ewell\n- **Ch. 19:** Tom Robinson's testimony (he helped Mayella out of pity; she kissed him)\n- **Ch. 20:** Atticus's closing argument — a powerful speech on equality before the law\n- **Ch. 21:** The jury returns a guilty verdict; the Black community in the balcony rises as Atticus passes\n\n> **Recommendation:** For the trial chapters specifically, your library's course reserves system or a licensed ebook platform will allow you to provide legitimate access to students.\n\n---\n\n## 5. 💻 Full Text for Computational Analysis\n\n⚠️ **Important — This request cannot be fulfilled, and here's why:**\n\nThe full-text retrieval tool returned a passage that does **not match** *To Kill a Mockingbird* (it features invented character names like \"Joshua Hartwell\" and \"Mr. Ridley\"). No legitimate, complete text of the novel was retrieved.\n\nMore importantly: *To Kill a Mockingbird* is **under copyright**. Downloading and loading a full unauthorized copy into a course platform — even for academic purposes — would constitute **copyright infringement** under the DMCA and U.S. Copyright Act. Fair use protections do not typically extend to reproducing entire works.\n\n**Compliant alternatives for computational text analysis:**\n1. **License a digital edition** through your institution's library (many ebook licenses permit computational/text mining use — ask your digital librarian).\n2. **HathiTrust Research Center (HTRC)** — offers a \"Non-Consumptive Research\" mode where computational analysis can be performed on in-copyright texts *without* downloading the raw text.\n3. **Use a public-domain surrogate** for method-testing (e.g., early Twain or Chesnutt works), then apply licensed access for the actual Mockingbird analysis.\n4. Contact the **rightsholder (HarperCollins)** for an academic research license.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Summary Table for Your Syllabus Build\n\n| Task | Status | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Publication history & controversy | ✅ Complete | Rich ban/challenge history; good for critical discussion |\n| Plot & themes | ✅ Complete | 6 major themes identified |\n| Online library availability | ✅ Complete | In-copyright; legal licensed access required |\n| Trial chapters | ⚠️ Partial | Chapter map provided; text retrieval returned wrong source |\n| Full text for analysis | ❌ Not provided | Copyright protected; use HTRC or license |",
